Two behaviors worth calling out:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mc2Maxv[0m/[38;5;10mc2Minv[0m use [38;5;10mif a > b { a } else { b }[0m rather than [38;5;10mf32::max[0m/[38;5;10mf32::min[0m. The C ternary returns the second operand when either side is NaN; [38;5;10mf32::max[0m returns the non-NaN operand instead. Using the intrinsics would have silently changed clamp results.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mc2Dot[0m reproduces the C compiler's exact SSE operand order ([38;5;10mmulss dst=b.y, src=a.y[0m, then [38;5;10maddss dst=(a.y*b.y), src=(a.x*b.x)[0m) through explicit [38;5;10mmulss[0m/[38;5;10maddss[0m/[38;5;10msubss[0m helpers that model x86 NaN priority — destination operand wins, quieted — plus the [38;5;10m0xFFC00000[0m indefinite for invalid operations. Without this, NaN inputs produced a differently-signed NaN than the C, visible as [38;5;10m-nan[0m vs [38;5;10mnan[0m.[0m[0m
